WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.629 --> 00:00:08.502
In 2018, over 600,000
people in the United States

00:00:08.502 --> 00:00:10.930
were charged for possessing marijuana.

00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:13.870
Not for running some
massive weed drug cartel.

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:15.690
For simply having it on them.

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:19.940
And despite making up only
31% of the population,

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:21.440
blacks and Latinos accounted

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:24.230
for nearly half of all weed arrests.

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:26.990
At the same time, the US cannabis industry

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:29.490
was and still is exploding.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:32.180
And between 80% to 90% of the industry

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:33.750
is run by white owners.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:35.940
So, not only are blacks and Latinos

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:37.230
more likely to get in trouble

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:39.010
for selling and having weed,

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:40.610
now that it's becoming legal,

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:43.220
they're nearly shut out of the industry.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:44.530
Somewhere along the line,

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:47.300
legal weed became a rich white business.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.980
So, how did this happen?

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:54.090
Cannabis' history of legality
is deep-rooted in racism.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.240
Back in the 1800s, the plant was used

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:59.290
for paper, textiles, and medicine.

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:01.720
But when Mexican migrants sought refuge

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:04.210
in the United States in the early 1900s,

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:05.730
they brought with them the practice

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:08.740
of smoking the plant, which
they called marijuana.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:11.140
A xenophobic campaign followed suit.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:13.570
The Great Depression's
widespread unemployment

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:15.950
heightened fears of Mexican immigrants,

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.810
and poor research linked cannabis

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:20.370
with violence and social deviance.

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:23.400
In 1936, a film titled "Reefer Madness"

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.440
depicted the drug as the
cause of sexual assault,

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:30.010
murder, and basically
complete teenage destruction.

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:32.450
Clip: Marijuana, the burning weed

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:34.671
with its roots in hell.

00:01:35.775 --> 00:01:38.490
Narrator: In 1937, Federal
Bureau of Narcotics

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:40.370
commissioner Harry Anslinger

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:42.970
brought forth the Marihuana Tax Act.

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:45.940
Yeah, he spelled it with an H.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:48.400
He used unsupported claims to blame weed

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:50.280
for the cause of social destruction

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.433
and linked it with blacks and Latinos.

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:57.270
The 1951 Boggs Act created
mandatory sentencing

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:59.200
for drug-related offenses.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.280
Congress repealed them in 1970,

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.060
but Presidents Nixon and Reagan

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:04.910
reintroduced them in new ways

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:06.820
in the later '70s and '80s.

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:09.090
During those decades, minority communities

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:12.090
were hit extra hard by over-policing.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:14.480
One of Nixon's top advisers admitted

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:16.470
that the war on drugs was a tactic

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:20.980
to bring down "the antiwar
left and black people."

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.340
And heavy policing in minority communities

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:24.520
is still rampant.

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:26.880
Queen Adesuyi: We know that
the case with Philando Castile,

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.130
part of the justification for the officer

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:31.500
claiming fear for his life

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.670
was because the car
smelled like marijuana.

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:35.730
How can we in the same breath

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:37.860
bring forth an industry that's making

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:40.200
extremely wealthy white men more wealthy

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.320
without considering how
we can repair those harms?

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:44.980
Narrator: Making matters worse,

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:47.010
people who've gone to prison for weed

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:48.620
are barred from selling it.

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:50.090
Even legally.

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:53.120
That's a huge problem for
someone like Edward Forchion.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:55.500
Edward Forchion: They're making
it legal for corporations.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:58.230
Guys like me, who went to
prison for selling weed,

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:01.070
are watching as rich white guys

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:03.036
are now making millions doing

00:03:03.036 --> 00:03:06.370
the exact same thing I went to prison for.

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:09.245
Narrator: Forchion has been a
weed enthusiast for decades,

00:03:09.245 --> 00:03:11.760
seeing its potential long
before medical marijuana

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:13.820
became legal in New Jersey.

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:15.600
He's opened a weed-themed restaurant

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:17.610
with a smoke shop next door.

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:20.640
He also ran for the 2019
New Jersey State Assembly

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:23.630
under his self-made
Legalize Marijuana Party.

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:26.720
He got 4.2% of the vote.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:28.980
But he's been arrested for weed-related

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:30.650
offenses in the past.

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:34.310
Forchion: On November
24, 1997, I was arrested.

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:36.350
It was a first-degree
charge for possession

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:38.687
with intent to distribute and conspiracy.

00:03:38.687 --> 00:03:39.720
Narrator: He took a plea deal,

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:42.040
but he was facing 20 years.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:46.630
Forchion: In 2010, I was
convicted of simple possession.

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:48.350
And, normally, no one goes to prison

00:03:48.350 --> 00:03:51.450
for simple possession,
but I got nine months.

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:54.830
Narrator: Now that medical
weed is legal in his state,

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:57.220
he's completely shut out of the business.

00:03:57.220 --> 00:03:59.800
Forchion: So, Debbie Madaio's my partner.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:02.710
Debbie, she has no
felonies, I have a felony.

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:05.680
Debbie could probably
go through the process

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.210
to get ownership, but just my name,

00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:10.670
just the fact that I
would be on the paperwork,

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:12.670
would invalidate her application.

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:14.740
Just the fact that I'm a felon,

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:16.640
I can't be on the paperwork.

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:17.700
Narrator: In other words,

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:20.920
if his name can't be on
the dispensary paperwork,

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:23.220
he wouldn't have official ownership.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:26.380
In protest, Forchion is
selling cannabis illegally

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:28.360
out of his adjacent smoke shop,

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:30.853
which is right across the
street from City Hall.

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:34.590
He's using the hashtag
#SellingWeedLikeImWhite,

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:37.220
practically daring the city to arrest him.

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:40.650
Forchion: You know, you can't
change laws or move the line

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:42.380
without stepping over the line.

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:44.630
I'm a big believer in civil disobedience.

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:47.980
I welcome whatever challenge the state

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:49.440
comes at me with.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:50.950
Narrator: But what about states

00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:53.570
that are more weed-friendly,
like Colorado?

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:56.700
They legalized recreational
marijuana in 2014,

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:00.220
and by 2017, they passed a
law that made it possible

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:02.040
for felons with marijuana convictions

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:03.390
to get their records cleared.

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:05.000
That's great, right?

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.670
Turns out getting the
record scrubbed clean

00:05:07.670 --> 00:05:09.390
isn't as simple as it seems,

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:11.510
and that's something dispensary owner

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:14.150
Wanda James knows all about.

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:15.433
Wanda James: For us, it became more of a

00:05:15.433 --> 00:05:16.508
political statement.

00:05:16.508 --> 00:05:17.732
There have been members of my family

00:05:17.732 --> 00:05:18.630
that have been arrested.

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:20.930
My brother did 10 years because of

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:22.993
a small amount of cannabis, 4 ounces,

00:05:22.993 --> 00:05:24.918
and for that he ended up picking cotton

00:05:24.918 --> 00:05:26.710
every day for four years in Texas.

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:28.710
This was shocking to
me when I learned this.

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:30.750
While we've always known it was illegal,

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:32.750
I'd never known that it was punitive.

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:34.610
In other words, everybody
that I've ever known

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:37.210
that's been caught here in
Colorado, i.e. white folks,

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:38.740
it's been mostly, "Give us that,

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:40.320
we're gonna throw it away," you know,

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:42.042
"Don't let me catch you
smoking pot anymore."

00:05:42.042 --> 00:05:43.950
You know, it's always been a warning.

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:45.000
Narrator: The shock of her brother's

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.010
arrest and sentencing inspired her

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:49.650
not only to open a dispensary,

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:51.190
but to help other minorities trying

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:52.890
to break into the business.

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:57.000
Out of the over 2,000 licensed
dispensaries in Colorado,

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:59.040
James estimates that less than 20

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:01.260
are owned by blacks and Latinos.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:03.740
And, as the first African American

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:05.910
to own a dispensary in the state,

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:07.900
James says one of the biggest roadblocks

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:10.690
is getting weed convictions cleared.

00:06:10.690 --> 00:06:13.290
In 2017, Colorado passed a bill

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:16.327
that could seal records of
misdemeanor cannabis use

00:06:16.327 --> 00:06:18.350
and possession convictions.

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:21.860
But sealing the records
wouldn't completely erase them.

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:24.680
It would just make them
unavailable to the public.

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:26.720
In other words, a potential employer

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:29.350
could see that you were
convicted of a misdemeanor

00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:31.300
but wouldn't know why.

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:33.820
Colorado's Turn Over a New Leaf Program

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:35.680
allows those with past convictions

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:38.300
to apply to have their records vacated,

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:41.690
which would dismiss the
charges and seal the record.

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:46.220
But Colorado's processes
are in no way automatic.

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:49.040
Out of the 17,000 people eligible,

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:51.680
only 71 people have cleared their records

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:53.560
as of October 2019.

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:56.510
The process requires digging
into all of your records,

00:06:56.510 --> 00:06:58.900
which can be difficult to track down.

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:01.117
Not to mention, any cases that don't

00:07:01.117 --> 00:07:03.530
specifically consist of marijuana charges

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:04.810
would be ineligible.

00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:08.190
So, if your charge is for
drug paraphernalia possession

00:07:08.190 --> 00:07:10.570
and you can't find the record that says

00:07:10.570 --> 00:07:13.010
"marijuana" paraphernalia possession,

00:07:13.010 --> 00:07:15.020
the record itself would be unclear

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:16.340
and you could have a hard time

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:17.720
getting the record erased.

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:19.815
Adesuyi: The biggest issues
faced by people of color

00:07:19.815 --> 00:07:21.250
in the industry is that when people

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:22.770
are rolling back prohibition,

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:24.670
there hasn't been a focus on censoring

00:07:24.670 --> 00:07:26.220
of communities most impacted.

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:27.760
James: All of those people that want

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:31.550
to have their records expunged or sealed

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:33.780
have to go through a process to do so.

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:35.460
You have to hire an attorney;

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:37.330
you have to meet with the AG

00:07:37.330 --> 00:07:39.160
of the state that you are in

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:41.840
to see if they will seal
or expunge your record.

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:43.150
Narrator: Legal fees are just one

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:44.910
of the many costs associated

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:47.023
with starting a legal cannabis business.

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:51.010
For communities that haven't benefited

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:53.380
from generational wealth and support,

00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:56.950
securing the necessary
capital is difficult.

00:07:56.950 --> 00:07:58.310
Throughout American history,

00:07:58.310 --> 00:08:01.180
minority communities have
been heavily policed,

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:03.810
denied equal pay and job opportunities,

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:06.040
forced into neighborhoods
through redlining,

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:07.170
and then forced out of those

00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:09.480
same neighborhoods through gentrification.

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:12.750
Amber Littlejohn: What we see
as the biggest stumbling block

00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:15.040
is actually access to capital

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:18.000
and necessary to fund the exorbitant costs

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:19.690
of participating in this industry.

00:08:19.690 --> 00:08:21.070
Narrator: Since weed is still illegal

00:08:21.070 --> 00:08:24.180
on the federal level,
prospective dispensary owners

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:26.550
can't get a bank loan to open a business.

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:28.890
Meanwhile, big hedge
funds have been investing

00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:32.170
in weed since before it
was even officially legal.

00:08:32.170 --> 00:08:34.300
And this has set up the biggest players

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:36.210
in the game for success.

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:38.110
Take MedMen, for instance.

00:08:38.110 --> 00:08:39.990
MedMen is a high-end dispensary

00:08:39.990 --> 00:08:42.400
operating mainly out of California,

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:44.900
where recreational cannabis is legal.

00:08:44.900 --> 00:08:46.960
It prides itself on basically being

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:48.340
the Apple store of weed.

00:08:48.340 --> 00:08:50.750
Attentive staff, sleek paraphernalia,

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:54.230
glass display cases, and
a superhigh price tag.

00:08:54.230 --> 00:08:56.820
MedMen was founded back in 2010,

00:08:56.820 --> 00:08:59.120
eight years before California allowed

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:01.730
licensed shops to sell recreational weed.

00:09:01.730 --> 00:09:03.280
Before the company went public,

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:08.130
it made $110 million in
private placement investments.

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:10.390
When profits dipped because of high taxes

00:09:10.390 --> 00:09:12.000
and dispensary restrictions,

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:15.100
it was able to secure a $100 million loan

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:17.740
from a cannabis-focused
investment company.

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:21.350
Today, the $1.6 billion company

00:09:21.350 --> 00:09:23.260
is continuing to expand at rates

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:25.350
faster than the legal system.

00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:27.800
Out of the six states it has offices in,

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:29.600
only three of those states have made

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:31.410
recreational cannabis legal.

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:34.630
If we look at small minority
businesses as a whole,

00:09:34.630 --> 00:09:36.410
they're statistically less likely

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:37.840
to receive investments.

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:41.370
Out of all venture-capital-backed
businesses in the US,

00:09:41.370 --> 00:09:43.780
only 1% were black-owned

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:46.410
and 1.8% were Latino owned.

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:47.900
This makes it hard for owners

00:09:47.900 --> 00:09:50.540
to hire more employees, expand,

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:53.110
or deal with unexpected profit losses.

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:55.680
For the weed industry, charges pile up

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:58.680
long before you're even
approved for a license.

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:01.100
By the time your application goes through,

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:03.670
you're looking at costs
within the millions.

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:06.920
Littlejohn: Starting simply
from the point of application,

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:10.152
we have some states where
the application fees

00:10:10.152 --> 00:10:13.400
themselves, for a single facility,

00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:15.570
are tens of thousands of dollars.

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:18.100
Now, that doesn't include the additional

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:20.890
capitalization requirements
that many states have

00:10:20.890 --> 00:10:24.250
that can enter into hundreds
of thousands of dollars

00:10:24.250 --> 00:10:26.410
per location and per license.

00:10:26.410 --> 00:10:28.443
James: When you're giving
out less than 60 licenses

00:10:28.443 --> 00:10:31.040
in a state or five licenses in a state,

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:33.747
it's going to cost you
anywhere between $50,000

00:10:33.747 --> 00:10:36.690
and $250,000 to have a technical writer

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:38.810
write your application for you.

00:10:38.810 --> 00:10:40.630
Narrator: While the
application is processing,

00:10:40.630 --> 00:10:42.210
you're also required to either own

00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:43.780
or rent a commercial property.

00:10:43.780 --> 00:10:45.320
This means, for the time it takes for you

00:10:45.320 --> 00:10:47.730
to get approved, you're
paying rent or mortgage

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:51.220
on a space you can't even
legally use for profit yet.

00:10:51.220 --> 00:10:54.960
Littlejohn: I talked to
a woman who lost her home

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:58.660
because she was trying to
finance her cannabis business

00:10:58.660 --> 00:11:00.603
and had to wait so long.

00:11:01.580 --> 00:11:04.310
Narrator: Current owners,
activists, and nonprofits

00:11:04.310 --> 00:11:06.590
are working to eliminate these roadblocks.

00:11:06.590 --> 00:11:09.522
But it all starts with people
in communities of color

00:11:09.522 --> 00:11:11.810
at the forefront of policy change.

00:11:11.810 --> 00:11:13.720
James: The reason why
it's extremely important

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:16.110
that we have black and brown ownership

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:20.350
is because this industry
was built on our back.

00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:23.160
So when you have 800,000 arrests a year

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:25.130
and 85% of them were black and brown

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:27.670
and we were targeted by police departments

00:11:27.670 --> 00:11:30.770
to work in privatized prison
systems as slave labor,

00:11:30.770 --> 00:11:32.980
we are owed these licenses.

00:11:32.980 --> 00:11:35.270
We are owed the opportunity to be owners

00:11:35.270 --> 00:11:36.470
in this industry.

00:11:36.470 --> 00:11:38.563
And states need to make that so.

